<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sol Plaatje</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1876">1876</a>–<a href="/years/1932">1932</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sol Plaatje was a South African journalist, writer and political leader, a founder and the first general secretary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">South African Native National Congress</a> in 1912. He went with its delegation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to protest against the Natives Land Act and described the law's effects in Native Life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> (1916). His Mhudi was one of the first novels in English by a black South African, and he translated Shakespeare into Setswana.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sol-plaatje.jpg" alt="Sol Plaatje" width="204" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sol_Plaatje_002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
